    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>This <strong>Cubii Pro review</strong> is for anyone weighing up Cubii's connected under desk elliptical: the model that pairs over Bluetooth with the Cubii app so your strides, distance and calories are logged without you writing anything down. You sit in your normal chair, pedal while you work, and the app keeps score.</p>
<p>The short answer: the Cubii Pro does the core job well. It is quiet, smooth and easy on the knees. The catch is that it can be hard to find new, and the newer [[amazon:cubii-jr2-plus|Cubii JR2+]] gives you the same Bluetooth tracking in a model that is usually easier to buy. Below we cover what owners like, what they complain about, and which Cubii makes sense for you, based on specs and owner reviews.</p>

<h2>What Is the Cubii Pro?</h2>
<p>The Cubii Pro is a compact seated elliptical. Two foot pedals move in an oval path driven by a magnetic flywheel inside a closed housing. A dial sets the resistance, and a small display shows your session stats. The "Pro" part is the Bluetooth connection: it syncs to the Cubii app, and from there your activity can flow into popular fitness trackers.</p>
<p>You will sometimes see it listed as the Cubii Pro HD or simply the "Bluetooth Cubii." Listings come and go, so treat any name variation with care and check what is actually in the box. If you want to see what is currently available, [[search:Cubii Pro under desk elliptical|search current Cubii Pro listings on Amazon]].</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Quick take:</strong> buy a connected Cubii if app tracking will keep you pedaling. If you just want to move your legs while you work, a non-Bluetooth model gets you the same ride for less.</p></div>

<h2>Cubii Pro Under Desk Elliptical: Setup and Daily Use</h2>
<p>There is very little to assemble. Owners typically attach the pedals, check the resistance dial turns freely, and pair the machine with the Cubii app. Pairing is the only step that occasionally takes a second try, and it usually comes down to phone Bluetooth permissions rather than the machine.</p>
<p>Day to day, the routine is simple. You slide the Cubii under your desk, sit back a little further than usual so your knees clear the desktop, and pedal at a low setting while you type. Most people find that light resistance lets them keep working without thinking about it, while higher settings are better saved for calls or reading. The app then shows your totals at the end of the day, which is where the Pro earns its keep compared with a basic model.</p>
<p>One honest note for this Cubii Pro review: the tracking is only as good as your habit of opening the app. If you know you will never look at it, you are paying for a feature you will not use.</p>

<h2>Cubii Pro Review: What Owners Like</h2>
<h3>It is genuinely quiet</h3>
<p>Magnetic resistance means no rubbing pads and no squeak when the machine is new. Owners regularly say they pedal through video calls without anyone noticing. That matters more than any spec, because a machine you have to stop using during meetings is a machine you stop using.</p>
<h3>The motion is easy on the knees</h3>
<p>An elliptical path keeps your feet on the pedals and avoids the sharp knee bend at the top of a bike stroke. That is why Cubii is popular with older buyers and people returning to exercise. Our guide to the <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> explains the difference in more detail.</p>
<h3>Tracking keeps people consistent</h3>
<p>The Bluetooth sync is the real reason to choose the Pro. Seeing a daily stride count and a weekly streak gives many people a reason to keep going after the novelty fades. Seated pedaling is often missed by wrist trackers, so having the machine report its own data is useful. If your tracker is the issue, read <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-count-as-steps/">does an under desk elliptical count as steps</a>.</p>
<h3>It fits under a typical desk</h3>
<p>Cubii keeps its machines low and compact, so most people can slide one under a standard-height desk. Taller users and low desks are the exception, which brings us to the downsides.</p>

<h2>Cubii Negative Reviews: The Honest Cons</h2>
<p>Every under desk elliptical gets complaints, and the Cubii ones are consistent enough to plan around. Here is what shows up most in Cubii negative reviews, and what to do about each.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Complaint</th><th>Why it happens</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>The machine creeps forward</td><td>Pushing pedals on a hard floor slides the base</td><td>Use a [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]] and brace it against a wall or desk leg</td></tr>
<tr><td>The chair rolls back</td><td>Office chair casters move instead of the pedals</td><td>Add [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]] or use a chair without wheels</td></tr>
<tr><td>Knees hit the desk</td><td>Knees rise at the top of each stride</td><td>Measure clearance first; sit a little further back</td></tr>
<tr><td>Top resistance feels light</td><td>It is built for all-day movement, not hard cardio</td><td>Pick a model with a wider range, such as the DeskCycle Ellipse</td></tr>
<tr><td>App pairing drops</td><td>Bluetooth and phone settings, not the flywheel</td><td>Re-pair in the app and keep the phone nearby</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>None of these are deal-breakers, but the top-resistance point is worth taking seriously. If you are already fit and want to break a sweat, Cubii is not the strongest choice. Our page on the <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-with-resistance/">under desk elliptical with the most resistance</a> covers better options for that goal.</p>

