<h2>Does Cubii Work? Realistic Results</h2>
<p>Cubii works if you judge it by the right yardstick. It adds light, low-impact movement to time you would otherwise spend sitting still. It does not turn your desk into a gym.</p>
<p>At a light seated pace, most people burn roughly 100 to 150 calories an hour, a bit more at higher resistance and higher body weight. That is modest per hour but meaningful across a workday, because it stacks on top of whatever exercise you already do. It is the same idea behind NEAT (non-exercise activity thermogenesis), the everyday movement that research associated with the Mayo Clinic has linked to differences in daily energy use. You can estimate your own numbers with our <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-calorie-calculator/">under desk elliptical calorie calculator</a>.</p>
<p>Owners also commonly report less afternoon stiffness and restless legs. That is a reasonable expectation for gentle movement, but if you are buying for a circulation or joint condition, check with your doctor first. For the broader picture, read <a href="/does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">does an under desk elliptical work</a>.</p>

<h2>Will a Cubii Fit Under Your Desk?</h2>
<p>Desk clearance is the most common reason any under desk elliptical goes back to the store. Your knees rise at the top of each stride, so a low desk, a tall chair or long legs can all cause contact. Measure before you order.</p>
[[tool:clearance]]
<p>If the checker says it will be tight, lower your chair a notch and sit slightly further back, or look at a lower-profile machine.</p>

<h2>Who Should Buy the Cubii Pro (and Who Shouldn't)</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Buy it if</strong> you like data, already use a fitness tracker, and want your desk pedaling counted automatically.</li>
<li><strong>Buy it if</strong> you need a quiet machine for calls and an easy, low-impact motion.</li>
<li><strong>Skip it if</strong> you want a hard workout. A higher-resistance machine or a walking pad suits you better.</li>
<li><strong>Skip it if</strong> you do not care about the app. Save money with the [[amazon:cubii-jr2|Cubii JR2]].</li>
<li><strong>Skip it if</strong> you plan to stand. Cubii models are seated only.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to Get More From a Cubii</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Set up once, properly.</strong> Mat under the machine, chair locked in place, display where you can glance at it.</li>
<li><strong>Use low resistance while typing.</strong> Save the higher settings for calls, reading and video.</li>
<li><strong>Pedal in blocks.</strong> Ten to fifteen minutes every half hour is easier to sustain than one long session. Our <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> handles the reminders.</li>
<li><strong>Check the app weekly.</strong> Look at the trend, not single days.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Verdict: Is Cubii Worth It?</h2>
<p>Our Cubii Pro review comes down to this: the connected Cubii is a quiet, comfortable, well-supported way to move while you work, and the app is a genuine motivator for people who like numbers. Its weak spots are a modest top resistance and the usual sliding issues, both easy to plan around.</p>
<p>Because the Pro is hard to find, most readers will be better served by the [[amazon:cubii-jr2-plus|Cubii JR2+]], which gives you the same Bluetooth idea in a current model. If you can live without the app, the standard JR2 is the best value in the lineup. Not sure which one fits you? Take the <a href="/tools/which-under-desk-elliptical-quiz/">60-second quiz</a> or compare everything in our <a href="/what-is-the-best-under-desk-elliptical/">best under desk elliptical</a> guide.</p>
